# family: session
# bridge: B(admission-trace)
# regime: exact (states, roles and peer ids are symbols and integers)
# instrument-first: 921cec0d
# baseline: tests/unit/session
# baseline-count: 38
#
# The unit is the lifecycle machine inside SessionCore, which is now
# NetwSessionCore: the states and their legal edges, the role peer identity and
# the configured hint resolve to, the join flood window, and the app-tag fold
# that gates admission.
#
# THE SAME TWO-PLANE SPLIT LIVENESS AND CLOCK TOOK, in a third place.
# SessionCore was never one class's worth of concern:
#
#   machine    the states, the edges, the roles, the flood window, the tag.
#              Arithmetic over integers and identities, no Object, no send.
#   interface  the authentication wire, the join payloads and their codec, the
#              nine frames replication_core.gd dispatches into, the persistence
#              flush a leave owes, and the awaits. GDScript until W4/W6.
#
# The MACHINE migrated completely, so the recipe's "a class migrates completely
# or not at all" holds on the class that crossed, and nothing straddles the two
# tiers.
#
# THE NAME. The native class is NetwSessionCore and the GDScript interface keeps
# the name SessionCore, the same disposition ClockCore took and for the same
# reason: State and Role are named from it in 413 places including the public
# twins on NetwMultiplayer. TRIGGER: rename to SessionShell when the interface
# crosses, where the sweep is already being paid.
#
# THE SCOPE IS NEW, AND THE MOVE WAS FORCED. Both suites lived in
# tests/unit/transport until this batch, carried by 38 rows of transport.ledger
# that already read "crosses with the session column". They had to move before
# anything could port: crossing-gate balances every ledger claiming a scope
# against that scope's whole census count, so a scope belongs to exactly one
# crossing family, and a second family porting a case out of tests/unit/transport
# would have made THAT ledger refuse however honest these rows were. The move,
# transport.ledger's shrink to 56, and the census retake landed together at
# fd443d0d and 8243c645, green before a line of C++ was written.
#
# WHAT FORCED WHAT, swept rather than assumed. Nothing outside session_core.gd
# named _LEGAL_EDGES, _join_stamps, _JOIN_RATE_LIMIT, _JOIN_TRACKED_PEERS,
# _on_enter_state or _on_exit_state, so the port deleted six members and swept
# zero files. The nine _handle_* receivers replication_core.gd dispatches into
# force NOTHING, exactly as clock's four did: each reaches api._replication, so
# each is interface-plane and stays GDScript beside the caller.
#
# THE SUBCLASS HAZARD IS WHY THE INTERFACE SURVIVES rather than being replaced.
# test_session_assignment_edge.gd declares `class _RecordingSession extends
# SessionCore` and overrides submit_join with a super call, which no GDScript
# subclass of a native class can do.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT is tests/crossing/session_arm.gd against
# tests/native/goldens/session_admission.txt: 66 rows over 17 scenarios,
# recorded at 921cec0d before a line of the machine was native, and red-proven
# four times across four independent halves (a flood limit one lower, the exit
# hook moved after the state write, a role hint ignored, a 16-bit tag mask). All
# 66 rows reproduced unchanged after the port.
#
# THE ARM REACHES TWO PRIVATES, deliberately, and both are behaviour rather than
# state. _join_flooded answers the guard's whole decision and has no public
# caller short of a real client submitting a real frame, and _compute_app_tag is
# the gate that decides whether two builds may talk at all. A port that folds it
# differently locks every peer out of a session that previously admitted them,
# which is worth more in a golden than the purity of not reaching for it.
#
# THE GOLDEN'S machine/ HALF IS THE ARM'S TO KEEP. session_trace_tests.cpp
# replays edges/, flood/ and apptag/, and stops there: the machine/ scenarios
# drive a real MultiplayerPeer through the interface, and replaying them
# natively would mean asserting what a peer answered rather than observing it.
# The arm keeps checking that half until the interface itself crosses.
#
# WHY ONLY THREE CASES PORTED, WHICH IS THE HONEST NUMBER. Almost every case in
# this scope drives auth, a join payload, a frame or the api's mirrored surface,
# and all of that is interface plane. The three that crossed are the three whose
# content is now entirely the machine's, and two of them needed LoopbackRig to
# cross at all: a fact-driven native case cannot cover the interface's own
# reading of a real peer, so R1 and R2 drive a real session instead. The arm is
# not run by any gate, so it is evidence and never a successor, and no row here
# names it.
#
# WHAT THE PORT GAINED, named because it is the reason the batch is worth more
# than its ported count. _join_flooded read the wall clock, so
# test_join_flood_guard_drops_past_the_window could count a flood and could
# never watch one expire, and the case that carried that name only ever checked
# the count. join_flooded takes the clock as an argument now, and S3 and S4
# prove the window expires and slides. The illegal edges gained the same:
# GDScript asserted on them, which aborts a debug build rather than reporting,
# so no arm could ever record the refusal, and S1 and S2 state it.
#
# CARRIED DEBT, named with its trigger. desired_role is pushed into the machine
# at every edge that can resolve a role rather than held there, because the
# configuration above is authored live and the value it carried at registration
# is not the one that decides. It becomes a plain member when NetwSessionConfig
# itself is native, at W6.
#
test_session_assignment_edge.gd:test_server_assignment_drives_online              -> session_rig_tests.cpp:"[Networked][Session] R1 a real server peer carries a real session to online at the assignment edge"
test_session_assignment_edge.gd:test_client_completes_online_on_connected_signal  -> session_rig_tests.cpp:"[Networked][Session] R2 a real client reaches online on the transport's own signal and reads as a client"
test_session_assignment_edge.gd:test_join_flood_guard_drops_past_the_window       -> session_core_tests.cpp:"[Networked][Session][Hosted] S3 the flood window expires, which is the half no wall clock could prove"
